<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sinn Féin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sinn Féin is an Irish republican political party founded by Arthur Griffith in 1905. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-rising">Easter Rising</a> it became the main nationalist party, winning most Irish seats in 1918 and setting up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dail-eireann">Dáil Éireann</a>. It split over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-irish-treaty">Anglo-Irish Treaty</a>, and later revivals of the name became linked with the Irish Republican Army; it is now a major party in both parts of Ireland.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name is Irish for Ourselves or We Ourselves, frequently mistranslated as ourselves alone, from the early 20th-century slogan Sinn Féin Amháin. It asserts Irish national <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> and self-determination rather than political union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> under the Westminster Parliament. The party was founded on 28 November 1905 when Arthur Griffith, at the first annual Convention of the National Council, outlined a policy to establish in Ireland's capital a national legislature endowed with the moral authority of the Irish nation. Its first platform was conservative and monarchist, advocating an Anglo-Irish dual monarchy modelled on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-compromise-of-1867">Austro-Hungarian Compromise of 1867</a>. It won 27 per cent of the vote in the 1908 North Leitrim by-election, after which support and membership fell.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Its members founded the revolutionary Irish Republic and its parliament, the First Dáil, and many were active in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-war-of-independence">Irish War of Independence</a>, when the party was associated with the Irish Republican Army. The party split before the Irish Civil War and again afterwards. Pro-Treaty deputies formed Cumann na nGaedheal in 1923, which governed the Irish Free State for nine years and merged with other groups to form Fine Gael in 1933. In 1926 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eamon-de-valera">Éamon de Valera</a>, after losing a motion to let elected members take their seats if the Oath of Allegiance were removed, resigned and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fianna-fail">Fianna Fáil</a>, taking most of the party's deputies with him. The remaining party was small, and for decades often without parliamentary representation.</p><p>A split in 1970, at the start of the Troubles, produced the modern party, linked in that conflict with the Provisional Irish Republican Army, and for most of it affected by broadcasting bans. Under Gerry Adams, leader from 1983, electoral politics became more important. The party dropped abstention from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dail-eireann">Dáil</a> in 1986, and some members formed Republican Sinn Féin in protest. In the 1990s Adams and Martin McGuinness took part in the Northern Ireland peace process leading to the Good Friday Agreement, and in 2006 the party signed the St Andrews Agreement and agreed to support the Police Service of Northern Ireland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Sinn Féin operates under democratic centralism, and describes itself as an Irish republican and democratic socialist party. It is active in both the Republic of Ireland and Northern Ireland, and its main political goal is a united Ireland. It continues a policy of abstaining from the House of Commons at Westminster. Caoimhghín Ó Caoláin, elected to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dail-eireann">Dáil</a> in 1997, was the first person under the Sinn Féin name to take a seat there since 1922. Mary Lou McDonald succeeded Gerry Adams as president in 2018.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sinn Féin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Easter Rising in Dublin in 1916 was quickly suppressed, but the execution of its leaders shifted opinion towards <b>Sinn Féin</b>, which won 73 of Ireland’s 105 seats in the general election of December 1918.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Already famous for his account of the Indian Rebellion of 1857, he studied in London between 1906 and 1910, where he worked out his idea of Hindu identity and befriended Indian student groups and others such as <b>Sinn Féin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/hindutva">Hindutva · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>During the War of Independence it was called the &quot;<b>Sinn Féin</b> anthem&quot;, and British forces confiscated copies as seditious; Éamon de Valera's platform at the East Clare by-election of June 1917 carried its opening lines on a banner.</blockquote><a href="/songs/amhran-na-bhfiann">Amhrán na bhFiann</a></li><li><blockquote>At a convention in Dublin, Arthur Griffith set out the <b>Sinn Féin</b> policy, meaning 'we ourselves', of Irish self-reliance and a separate Irish parliament.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-28">28 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Sinn Fein</b> won most Irish seats in the 1918 general election, its members met in Dublin on 21 January 1919 as Dail Eireann, adopted a Declaration of Independence and created a government, with Eamon de Valera as president from April 1919.</blockquote><a href="/state/irish-republic">Irish Republic</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sinn Fein</b> won 124 of the 128 seats unopposed but treated the election as one for the second Dail of the Irish Republic, so the Parliament of Southern Ireland met only twice, in June 1921 and January 1922.</blockquote><a href="/state/southern-ireland">Southern Ireland</a></li></ul></section>
